# Development values, committed deliberately, so `npm run dev` works on a
# fresh clone with no manual setup. See the note in the repo root .gitignore.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM .env.production
# ----------------------------------------------------
# Vite loads different files per mode:
#   npm run dev    -> mode "development" -> .env, .env.development
#   npm run build  -> mode "production"  -> .env, .env.production
# so a value in .env.production alone works in the deployed build and not in
# the dev server. That exact asymmetry cost this project an evening on
# 2026-09-24.
#
# WHY NOT JUST .env
# -----------------
# .env is gitignored and must stay that way -- it is where personal and
# sensitive values go. This file holds only a public URL that is substituted
# into the client bundle anyway, so there is nothing here to protect. Never add
# a secret to it.
#
# WHY NOT LEAVE IT BLANK LIKE VITE_API_URL
# -----------------------------------------
# VITE_API_URL is blank locally on purpose: empty makes the app call /api/...
# relatively, and vite.config.js proxies that to the local backend. Roktim has
# no proxy equivalent, because it deliberately does not go through the Node
# backend at all. For Roktim, empty simply means "absent", and every surface
# renders nothing -- silently, by design.
#
# TO OVERRIDE (e.g. running the forecast service locally on port 5002)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Create frontend/.env.development.local, which is gitignored and takes
# precedence over this file (verified):
#
#     VITE_ROKTIM_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5002
#
# Note the forecast service's ROKTIM_ALLOWED_ORIGINS must include
# http://localhost:5173 for the dev server to be allowed to read /forecast/*.

VITE_ROKTIM_URL=https://roktonet-forecast.onrender.com
